From b761a25b68e89a412f548e702857f667a0c028b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: milliluk-erik <build@localhost>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 11:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 49/49] joystick: Diecom RAT quadrature mouse (-rat-mouse)

The RAT wires two quadrature wheels onto a joystick port's analogue inputs.
From the machine's side it is a pot whose resistance snaps between four
discrete levels, one per wheel detent, and a driver recovers direction from
the sequence of levels rather than from any single reading.  Nothing in XRoar
could present that, so guest code that drives one -- Color Max Deluxe has a
RAT driver with its own GIME timer ISR -- had no way to be executed at all.

It is a filter over one port's axis readings, not a joystick module, and that
is the design decision worth defending.  A module supplies a position; the RAT
does not have one, because the position lives in the guest, which integrates
the detents.  Filtering inside joystick_read_axis() instead means the port
keeps whatever source it already had, so -joy-right script drives a RAT from
an input script, the mouse drives one under a GUI build, and neither had to
learn anything.  It also costs one branch on a path that already exists,
against a module's worth of duplicated plumbing.

The four levels are MAME's joy_rat_table[] = { 15, 24, 42, 33 }, six-bit DAC
codes in phase order, deliberately not monotonic: ordered by resistance the
phases run 0, 1, 3, 2, so consecutive phases are neighbours in the guest's
bucketed reading and one bucket of change decodes to a direction.  A code is
presented as the axis value that the comparator in each machine's
joystick_update() maps back to it -- ((v << 2) | 2) << 8, which is
joystick_dac_target_value()'s formula read backwards.

-rat-rate is the option that can be got wrong, so its default was chosen
against a real decoder rather than guessed.  Feeding Color Max Deluxe's own
decode table a run of detents: one per guest sample tracks exactly, one pixel
each.  TWO per sample while it is already moving decodes correctly as two
pixels; two per sample FROM REST decodes as no motion at all and leaves the
decoder's phase where it started, so a wheel that only ever steps two at a
time is invisible.  Three per sample decodes as a reversal.  Hence a ceiling
below the guest's sample rate: 150Hz by default, under both the ~157Hz the
original RAT2.BIN driver sampled at and the 180.5Hz GIME timer Color Max
Deluxe uses.  At one pixel per detent that crosses a 320-pixel screen in about
two seconds, which is what the device really costs.

The ceiling is enforced in emulated time and evaluated lazily in the read,
because a port is read far more often than it is sampled -- every $FF00 read
runs the comparator and the keyboard scan does that constantly -- so a counter
driven by reads would be driven by the keyboard.  A level can therefore change
in the middle of a guest's threshold compare; that is safe, because the
compare buckets monotonically and a one-detent move lands in one of the two
adjacent buckets, both legal transitions, and it is what real hardware does.

Buttons needed nothing: the RAT has two, a port already carries two, and
joystick_read_buttons() places all four where the CoCo 3 wires PIA0 PA0-PA3.

Absolute position does not survive the trip and a script should not assume it
does: the guest's decode hands out one or two pixels per detent depending on
its phase, and eats a detent whenever the direction reverses.  Drive to a hard
stop for a known origin and peek the guest's cursor rather than computing it.

Verified: builds with -Werror; -h and -rat-mouse help list it; the four levels
land one per bucket against the thresholds Color Max Deluxe's sampler strobes,
with guest code == phase, so a phase increment is a code increment.

Also verified against a guest now, which the first version of this message
said had not been done.  A 6809 program doing the same six-bit successive
approximation a real driver does -- SEL2/SEL1 low for the right port's X
axis, walk the DAC in PIA1 A bits 7..2, read the comparator on PIA0 PA7 --
and logging each code through emuext, driven by -input-script sweeps:

  -rat-mouse right, sweeping right   15 24 42 33 15 24 42 33 ...
  the same sweep leftward            15 33 42 24 15 33 42 24 ...
  no -rat-mouse (control)            31 38 39 46 54, tracking the axis

So the presented codes are exactly joy_rat_table[] in phase order, the
sequence reverses with direction, and the filter is inert when the option
is absent.  The leftward run needs the wheel to catch up with the source
first: a centre-to-limit jump is 160 detents, which at the 150Hz ceiling
takes about a second, and sampling before that finishes only ever shows
the wind-on.  That is the device being honest, not a bug -- see the note
above about absolute position not surviving the trip.
